The cost of taut planning is conditional on how much labour the economy can
mobilise: the same revision gain destroys 18.5 per cent of output when the economy
can employ only 5 per cent more labour than in the base year, 14.6 per cent at 15 per
cent, 0.4 per cent at 40 per cent, and nothing when labour is abundant. The controls
(gain 0 with balancing on, gain 1 with balancing off) return base-year output at every
level of slack, so the interaction rather than either factor is what costs. Source:
reference_results/ (the Lead's independent reference run; the experiment layer's
labour axis in results/B6 reproduces the same ordering).
